I. POLYMORPHISM
====================
--> came fromm Geek words -- 'poly' -- Many + 'morphos' -- forms
--> Polymorphism is the ability of an object or method to take many forms and exhibit different behavior
    in different situations.
 (The ability of a method to behave differently for different object ==> polymorphism)

 -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

 II. TYPES OF POLYMORPHISM
 =========================
 1. Compile Time Polymorphism (Static Polymorphism)
 ------------------------------------------------------------------------
Compile-time polymorphism is the ability of a method to perform different tasks based on different parameter lists.
The method to be executed is decided by the compiler during compilation.

 Method Overloading :
    In a class, having multiple methods with same name and with different parameters (no changing return type).
 --> Different parameters (Changing no of parameters)
 --> Changing Data types of parameters
 --> Changing order of parameters.

   ** Method overloading is used to increase the readability of the program and to handle different
    types of inputs with the same method name.


 * CTP achieved by Method Overloading (also known as early/static binding)

    Example :
    class Calculator {

        int add(int a, int b) {
            return a + b;
        }

        int add(int a, int b, int c) {
            return a + b + c;
        }
    }

    add(10, 20);      // calls first method
    add(10, 20, 30);  // calls second method

 WHY DO WE SAID - COMPILE TIME POLYMORPHISM
 -------------------------------------------
because the compiler determines which overloaded method should be executed
 during compilation itself, before the program runs.
 (Out of so many methods, which method implementation should be executed is decided by the compiler during the compile time).

 2. Run Time Polymorphism (Dynamic Polymorphism)
 --------------------------------------------------------------------------
 The process of one method behaving differently for the different objects during the run time.
            Reference Type = Parent
            Object Type = Child

            Example:
            Animal a = new Dog();

 Method Overriding :
    Method overriding is the process of providing a new implementation for an inherited method in the child class.
    --> same method name, same parameters , same return type.
    --> IS-A-RELATIONSHIP
    --> During Method Overriding, we can use optionally annotation "@Override" which determines the method is overridden
        for a specific method.
    --> Cannot override static, private, or final methods because they are either class-level or cannot
        be modified

  * Main Purpose
  --------------
  The main purpose of method overriding is to allow a subclass to modify or extend the behaviour
  of a method inherited from a superclass, enabling dynamic or runtime polymorphism.

  * RTP achieved by Method Overriding (also known as late/dynamic binding)

  Example :
    class Animal {
        void sound() {
            System.out.println("Animal");
        }
    }

    class Dog extends Animal {
        void sound() {
            System.out.println("Bark");
        }
    }

    Animal a = new Dog();
    a.sound();

WHY DO WE SAID - RUN TIME POLYMORPHISM
-------------------------------------------------
JVM decides which overridden method to execute based on the actual object created at runtime

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

III. DIFFERENCE BETWEEN METHOD OVERLOADING & OVERRIDING
==========================================================
----------------------------------------------------
| Method Overloading        | Method Overriding     |
| ------------------------- | --------------------- |
| Compile-Time Polymorphism | Run-Time Polymorphism |
| Same class                | Parent & Child class  |
| Different parameters      | Same parameters       |
| Inheritance not required  | Inheritance required  |
| Compiler decides          | JVM decides           |
-----------------------------------------------------
