IV. If java does not support polymorphism, how can we achieve the concept of a method to behave differently
for different objects?
--> could be achieved using conditional statements such as if else, switch, instanceof
--> This approach increases code complexity, reduces maintainability and requires modifying existing code
    whenever a new object type is created.
--> For instance, to execute different actions for Dog and Cat objects, the programmer would need to explicitly
    check the object type and call the corresponding method.

    Example:
    if(obj instanceof Dog)
        ((Dog)obj).sound();
    else if (obj instanceof Cat)
         ((Cat)obj).sound();

    But in the case of polymorphism is preferred because it automatically invokes the appropriate method at run time
    based on the actual object, making programs more flexible and extensible